                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#section_Conjunction"} Conjunction
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#section_Existential_quantifier"} Existential quantifier
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#section_Disjunction"} Disjunction
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#section_Excluded_middle"} Excluded middle
              %li
                %a{:href => "#section_Related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#section_References"} References
          %p
          %h2#section_Idea Idea
          %p
            Higher-order logic is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            which contains the notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+predicates"} higher-order predicates
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HOL4"} HOL4
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isabelle"} Isabelle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simple type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %p
            Dependent type theory already has the ability to form higher-order functions via iterated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+mathematics"} impredicative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+propositions"} type of all propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}
            in dependent type theory. The idea behind the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+propositions"} type of all propositions
            in dependent type theory is the principle of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+codes+for+subsingletons"} propositions as codes for subsingletons
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo :
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P:\mathrm{Prop}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}(P)
            and a witness that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}(P)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-propositions"} h-propositions
          %p
            Adding a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+propositions"} type of all propositions
            to dependent type theory is sufficient to construct all the usual first-order logical operations and values that are also required for higher-order logic, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"} truth
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/falsehood"} falsehood
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exclusive+disjunction"} exclusive disjunction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniqueness+quantifier"} uniqueness quantifier
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simple type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
          %p
            There are usually two ways to present a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hierarchy+of+universes"} hierarchy of universes
            and either no type judgments or an infinite number of type judgments to define types, and a second which uses no universes and a single type judgment to define types. Higher-order logic such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HOL"} HOL
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isabelle"} Isabelle
            has no infinite hierarchy of universes, so the only way to present higher-order logic as a dependent type theory is using a single type judgment.
          %p
          %h2#section_Presentation Presentation
          %p#JudgmentsAndContexts
          %h3#section_Judgments_and_contexts Judgments and contexts
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of higher-order logic as dependent type theory can be constructed in two stages:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The first is the
                %em raw
                or
                %em untyped
                syntax of the theory consisting of expressions that are readable but not necessarily meaningful.
            %li
              %p
                The second stage consists of defining the
                %em derivable judgements
                of the type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductively
                which then pick out the meaningful
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
            of succesively
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            may be defined as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Bruijn+indices"} de Bruijn indices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is given by
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
          %p
            One may also define contexts as coming with a variable name, in which case one needs a notion of
            = succeed "-equivalence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p Types and terms are built inductively from various constructors. Types, terms and contexts are defined mutually.
          %p
            We have the basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"} judgement
            forms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}
              %p
                saying that: “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is a well-formed context”;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\; \mathrm{type}
              %p
                saying that “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a well-typed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed "\u201D;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash a : A
              %p
                saying that “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is a well-typed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed "\u201D;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p
            We work in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+framework"} logical framework
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
          %p
            To start with,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
            are built from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            by the following rules (cf.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+telescope"} type telescope
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{}{() \; \mathrm{ctx}} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx} \quad \Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type}}{(\Gamma, a:A) \; \mathrm{ctx}}
          %p#StructuralRules
          %h3#section_Structural_rules Structural rules
          %p
            Among the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            of dependent type theory are (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Jacobs98"} Jacobs (1998), p. 122
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Rijke22"} Rijke (2022), Sec. 1.4
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variable+rule"} variable rule
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution+rule"} substitution rule
          %p
            The weakening and substitution rules are
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rules"} admissible rules
          %p
          %h3#section_Judgmental_equality Judgmental equality
          %p In addition, there are judgments for judgmental equality of types and of terms:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A \equiv A' \; \mathrm{type}
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
              are judgementally equal well-typed types in context
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash a \equiv a' : A
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
              are judgementally equal well-typed terms of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in context
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p Judgmental equality has its own structural rules: reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity, the principle of substitution, and the variable conversion rule.
          %ul
            %li Reflexivity of judgmental equality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma \vdash A \equiv A \; \mathrm{type}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a:A}{\Gamma \vdash a \equiv a:A}
          %ul
            %li
              %p Symmetry of judgmental equality
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi B
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma \vdash B \equiv A \; \mathrm{type}}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a \equiv b:A}{\Gamma \vdash b \equiv a:A}
            %li
              %p Transitivity of judgmental equality
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi B
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi C
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi C
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash B \equiv C \; \mathrm{type} }{\Gamma \vdash A \equiv C \; \mathrm{type}}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi c
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi c
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
